1. Recursion vs. Iteration(divided and conquer)
While recursion is the natural choice for Divide and Conquer because it maps directly to the problem's mathematical structure and makes code 
significantly easier to read and maintain, I prefer iteration if the problem requires deep recursion where I anticipate stack limitations.
In production code, if the depth of recursion is predictable and moderate, I choose recursion for readability. If I am building a high-performance 
library where stack depth might exceed JVM limits, I would consider converting the recursive logic into an iterative one by using an explicit 
Deque to manage the sub-problems manually.

2. LinkedHashMap vs. HashMap(Key-value/insertion order or not)
The primary difference lies in the ordering of elements.
HashMap does not guarantee any order of its entries; it is optimized for high-performance retrieval and insertion using hash buckets.
LinkedHashMap, on the other hand, extends HashMap and maintains a doubly-linked list that runs through all of its entries. This allows it to 
preserve the insertion order by default. Additionally, LinkedHashMap can be configured to maintain access order, which makes it an ideal data 
structure for implementing LRU caches.
In summary, I use HashMap by default for better memory efficiency, and switch to LinkedHashMap only when the requirement specifies that the 
iteration order must match the insertion or access order.

3. Grouping People by Age Link<People> list ->stream API /for loop -> list.stream().collect(Collectors.groupingBy(Person::getAge))
In modern Java (8+), the most elegant and standard way to achieve this is by using the Stream API combined with Collectors.groupingBy.
You can simply write: people.stream().collect(Collectors.groupingBy(Person::getAge)).
This method creates a Map<Integer, List<Person>> in a single statement. It is much cleaner and less error-prone than the traditional loop approach.
If I were using a version prior to Java 8, I would use a for-each loop with map.computeIfAbsent(age, k -> new ArrayList<>()).add(p), which is the 
idiomatic way to handle missing keys efficiently.

4. New Features in Java 8 11 17 21
In my view, Java’s evolution has been focused on developer productivity and scalability. Each LTS release has added specific tools to solve the 
challenges of its time:
Java 8 revolutionized the language by introducing Lambda expressions and the Stream API, enabling functional programming patterns that simplified 
collection processing.
Java 11 focused on modernization and developer convenience, bringing in the standardized HTTP Client API and local-variable type inference (var), 
which reduced boilerplate code significantly.
Java 17 improved maintainability and data modeling with the introduction of Records for immutable data carriers and Sealed Classes to restrict class 
hierarchies, alongside pattern matching for instanceof.
Java 21 is a game-changer with Virtual Threads, which finally solves the throughput limitations of traditional thread-per-request models by allowing 
millions of lightweight threads to run concurrently.
To summarize, these incremental updates have consistently pushed Java toward becoming more concise, type-safe, and highly scalable for modern 
cloud-native architectures.

5. Writing REST API in Spring Boot
To design a robust REST API in Spring Boot, I follow a standard layered architecture:
Controller Layer: I use @RestController to handle HTTP requests. I map endpoints using @GetMapping, @PostMapping, etc., and use ResponseEntity to return 
appropriate HTTP status codes (like 200 OK or 201 Created).
Service Layer: This is where the business logic resides. By keeping it separated from the controller, I ensure that the code is testable and maintainable.
Repository Layer: I leverage Spring Data JPA, which significantly reduces boilerplate code for database interactions.
For API design, I strictly follow RESTful conventions: using nouns for resource naming, using appropriate HTTP methods (GET, POST, PUT, DELETE), 
and ensuring that the API is stateless. I also implement global exception handling using @ControllerAdvice to ensure consistent error responses across the
entire application.

6. How did you debug?(check the log for that description(http status, log error/exception)->request -> reproduce(prod)->dev(request payload + postman + 
local intelliJ) -> debug + breakpoint
My debugging process is highly structured. When a production issue is reported, I don't jump straight into the code.
First, I examine the logs and metrics to identify the HTTP status codes and the specific exception stack traces. This tells me 'what' happened and 'where' 
it occurred.
Second, I extract the exact request payload that triggered the failure. I then aim to reproduce the issue in my local environment or a sandbox using Postman,
feeding it the captured payload to see if the error persists.
Once reproduced, I use the IntelliJ debugger to set breakpoints at the point of failure. This allows me to inspect the object states, call stacks, and logical
flows in real-time. If it's a performance-related issue, I would further use a profiler (like VisualVM or JProfiler) to analyze memory heaps or thread contention.
By following this methodical approach—from log analysis to local reproduction and breakpoint debugging—I minimize the time taken to root-cause issues while 
ensuring the fix I implement is precise.

7. Design the locking schema so that when a thread call method1(), it needs to until some other thread call method2()
To implement this using wait() and notify(), I use a private lock object to manage the state.
State Management: I define a boolean flag isMethod2Called.
The Wait Logic: In method1, I wrap the wait() call inside a synchronized block. Crucially, I use a while loop to check the flag: while (!isMethod2Called) 
{ lock.wait(); }. This is essential to prevent spurious wakeups.
The Notify Logic: In method2, I update the flag to true and call notify() within a synchronized block to signal the waiting threads.
Using a while loop instead of an if statement is a best practice here to ensure that the condition is re-validated after the thread is awakened.

